hadiths[count]{hadithnumber,text}:
"1514","Narrated to us Muhammad bin Abdul-Ala al-San’ani, narrated by al-Mu’tamir, who said: I heard Humaid, who said: Bakr told me, on the authority of Hamza bin al-Mughirah bin Shu’bah, on the authority of his father, that the Prophet, may God’s prayers and peace be upon him, “left behind, and Al-Mughirah ibn Shu’bah left behind with him,” so he mentioned the hadith in its length, and said: He said: So we reached the people, and Abd al-Rahman ibn Awf had prayed a rak’ah. When he sensed the coming of the Prophet, may God bless him and grant him peace, he went to stay behind. Then the Prophet, may God’s prayers and peace be upon him, motioned to him to pray.” When Abdul-Rahman finished praying and gave peace, “the Prophet, may God’s prayers and peace be upon him, and Al-Mughirah stood up.” So they completed what came before them.” Abu Bakr said: This term may be mistaken by those who do not reflect on this issue and do not understand science and jurisprudence. Some of those who According to the doctrine of the Iraqis, he says that when he completed his last prayer with the imam, this word indicates that the Prophet, may God bless him and grant him peace, And Al-Mughirah only performed the first rak’ah; Because Abdul Rahman only preceded them with the first, not with the second, and likewise they claimed in the saying of the Prophet, may God bless him and grant him peace: “And whatever you miss, make up for it.” They claimed that it indicates that he only makes up the first of his prayers, not the last, and this interpretation, whoever ponders jurisprudence, knows that this interpretation Contrary to what all the people of prayer say, since if the Chosen One, may God bless him and grant him peace, and Al-Mughirah, after the peace of Abd al-Rahman ibn Awf, had decided The first rak'ah that they missed, they would have performed a rak'ah without sitting or reciting the tashahhud, since the rak'ah that they missed was the first prayer. Abd al-Rahman bin Awf, it was a rak’ah without sitting, and without reciting the Tashahhud. In the agreement of the people of prayer, the one who catches up with the imam for one rak’ah of the Fajr prayer should perform one rak’ah while sitting, reciting the tashahhud, and saying salaam. It is clear and authentic that the Prophet May God’s prayers and peace be upon him, he did not perform the first rak’ah in which there was no sitting, nor the tashahhud, nor salutations, but he performed the second rak’ah in which there was sitting. And the tashahhud and peace, even if the meaning of his saying, may God’s prayers and peace be upon him: “And whatever you missed, make up for it,” means: make up for what you missed, as those who claimed it He disagreed with us on this issue. Whoever missed a rak’ah of prayer with the imam had to make up a rak’ah by standing, bowing, and prostrating without Sitting, no tashahhud, no salutations. And in their agreement with us, he performs a rak’ah by sitting and saying the tashahhud, since it has been proven that sitting, tashahhud, and saying salaam are among the rulings of the last rak’ah. Not from the ruling of the first. Whoever understands knowledge and has reason, and is not arrogant, knows that there is no tashahhud, no sitting for the tashahhud, and no salaam in the first rak’ah of the prayer."
